Almanac: {
	FlavorText: {
		// Conventional chemistry
		Sulfur: Brimstone scraped off the ash where the world runs hot. Smells like a promise it fully intends to keep!
		Niter: On its own it does nothing at all. But set it beside something that burns, and watch it turn polite company into an argument. Hehe.
		RockSalt: Boil the brine and this is what you get! Preserves, scours, and puts out fires.
		PureSalt: Crust from the mixture of brine and fresh water. More potent than rock salt, and it knows it.
		Quicklime: Limestone burnt past giving a crap. Give it water and it gives you heat back, immediately and without room for discussion.
		BoneAsh: A good ol' residue that you oughta handle respectfully; it's already been through enough.
		Potash: Wood ash, wetted and dried. It's of the humblest things in this book, and half the almanac calls it a friend.
		// Acids.
		OilofVitriol: Green stone roasted until it weeps like a baby. Careful! It'll weep on you too if you're careless.
		AquaFortis: Folks call this 'strong water'; It will take apart every metal you own but one, that's for you to find out though.
		SpiritofSalt: The salt's been persuaded to become a fume. I could never really explain what it's real purpose is, so you'll have to find out yourself.
		AquaRegia: Two acids that could not manage it alone. Together they dissolve even gold, which ought to tell you something.
		BlueVitriol: Copper drowned in vitriol comes back blue. It's useful, and prettier than it has any need to be.
		// Alkalis.
		SlakedLime: Quicklime that's quarrelled with water. Although, this one puts fires out instead of starting them.
		CausticLye: It cleans by unmaking things. Do not test which of you it considers dirt.
		FrigorificSalt: Mix these and the bottle pulls the warmth right out of your hand! It's cold you make rather than find.
		// Distillates and powders.
		SpiritofOil: The first and lightest thing off the still, and the most eager to catch. Keep it away from the lamp, neph.
		Pitch: A sticky residue you get out of crude oil. Sticks to whatever it lands on; I wouldn't recommend falling into it.
		BlackPowder: Six of niter to one of sulfur to one of coal. Get the ratio wrong and it merely smoulders. Get it right and stand further back!
		ColdFire: It lights itself. No spark, no flint, only air! Store it under water and think about that.
		// Reagents the book had somehow never listed.
		BottledOil: Thick, dark, and patient. Everything it touches becomes easier to set ablaze. Use with caution! I know how you like to play with fire.
		BrineBottle: Salt water in a bottle. I wouldn't call it glamorous, but boil it down and you have the start of a salt pipeline.
		FibrousSlurry: A keen solvent made of cobwebs and plant matter. Thickens, binds, and asks for nothing in return.
		OxidizedCopper: Copper that has been left alone with the weather too long. The green is the interesting part!
		RustedNugget: Iron that's had it's butt kicked by air overtime. The rust is the real alchemist here.
		// Blocks
		DirtBlock: Just dirt. But it has a story, and a place in the world. Don't forget that.
		MudBlock: Dirt, and the water that made it. It has a story, and a place in the world. Don't forget that.
		SandBlock: Most glance at it and see nothing. But sand's got a purpose. Keep it in mind, you'll see that primitive things can be mighty useful.
		EbonsandBlock: Sand that's been through a lot. The corruption's left it baneful, which actually works in your favor.
		CrimsandBlock: Sand that's been through a lot. The crimson's left it baneful, which actually works in your favor.
		SnowBlock: The weather can be an alchemist too, snow's a perfect example. This water-soluble's got heart, let it melt and you'll see what I mean.
		// Herbs
		Daybloom: A flower that opens with the sun. It likes plain soil, and is quite charasmatic.
		Magnolia: A mentor of the plant world. Magnolies have special pheromones that make other herbs wise up. Everyone wants these around.
		Moonglow: A flower that opens with the moon. It likes mud, and it likes to be left alone.
		Blinkroot: Blinkroots are shy until mature. They're easy to miss until they start illuminating the caverns. If you need a good catalyzer, these are your goto.
		Waterleaf: Waterleafs are picky about their soil. They need conflict, which is why they grow on sand. If you need to dilute somethin', they'll be your handyman.
		Shiverthorn: Shiverthorns are a hardy herb. They like to be left alone, and they like the cold. If you need a soft catalyst, these are your goto.
		Fireblossom: Hot and fiery! These plants like ash, and have a burning passion for life. If you need somethin' volatile, these are your goto.
		Sunflower: Always up bright and early, facing the sun. Sunflowers are a good source of oil, but make sure to treat them right.
		Deathweed: Deathweeds are the only herb that can grow in the twisted biomes! They also vitiate the soil around them, so be careful where you plant them.
		// Mushrooms
		Mushroom: Your standard fungus. 'Shrooms are a good start to alchemy, but they don't have much to say for themselves. Argie would say otherwise.
		GlowingMushroom: A mushroom that glows in the dark. Here's a secret: the glow is actually a chemical reaction!
		FlyAgaric: A mushroom that can make you see things. It's a good catalyst, but don't get too carried away with it.
		VileMushroom: Poison! I've tried eating one like a fool. Wouldn't recommend it. If you need a good poison, these are your goto.
		ViciousMushroom: As the name suggests! They're quite vicious, and useful for making poisons.
		// Misc
		AntlionMandible: The jaw of a desert antlion. It's sharp, and a solid reagent.
		Amber: Fossilized tree resin. A good way to make fire. Many like the fancy of it.
		Cobweb: A spider's home. It's sticky, and a good solute.
	}
}
